use std
into ofcrypt.amni

This file is a module with no main (namespace ofcrypt). It decrypts password-protected Office Open XML files (.docx, .xlsx and .pptx saved "with a password to open"), as [MS-OFFCRYPTO] defines them: the file is a compound file (see `cfb`) with the streams "EncryptionInfo" and "EncryptedPackage", and the encrypted package is the ordinary ZIP of the document. It supports Agile encryption (EncryptionInfo version 4.4 with an XML descriptor; the hash SHA1, SHA256, SHA384 or SHA512; AES-128, AES-192 or AES-256 in CBC mode) and Standard encryption (version 2.2, 3.2 or 4.2 with AES, SHA-1, ECB mode, the 50000-round key derivation); RC4 CryptoAPI, external and extensible encryption, certificate-only key encryptors and everything else are unsupported. The data integrity HMAC of Agile files is not checked. Every module-level name starts with `oc_` or `OC_`.

```amni
import cfb;
import xml;
import dcrypt;
import base64;
import crypto.hash;
import crypto.aead;
```

Helpers from other modules, used exactly as described here. `cfb::cf_open(data const byte[:]) int` returns a handle or -1; `cfb::cf_find(h int, path const byte[:]) int` returns the entry index or -1; `cfb::cf_is_stream(h int, i int) bool`; `cfb::cf_get(h int, path const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` APPENDS the stream's bytes to out and returns true (false, leaving out as it was, when the stream is missing or unreadable); `cfb::cf_close(h int)`; `cfb::cf_le(s const byte[:], p int, n int) int` reads an unsigned little-endian number of n bytes (1 to 8) at offset p of s and returns -1 when p is negative, p plus n passes the end of s, or the value does not fit in 63 bits. `xml::xm_parse(data const byte[:])` replaces the one parsed document; `xml::xm_count() int` is its number of events; `xml::xm_find(from int, to int, local const byte[:]) int` is the index of the first element at or after from and before to whose local name (the part after any colon) is local, or -1; `xml::xm_child(i int, local const byte[:]) int` is the first direct child element of element i with that local name, or -1; `xml::xm_attr(i int, local const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` CLEARS out and, when element i has an attribute with that local name, stores its decoded value and returns true (false otherwise). `base64::decode(out byte[>..]&, src const byte[:]) bool` APPENDS the decoded bytes of src to out and returns true; it returns false, leaving out as it was, when src is not valid base64 (its length must be a multiple of 4). `crypto::digest(kind int, d const byte[:]) byte[>..]` returns the hash of d, where kind is `crypto::SHA1` (1, a 20-byte digest), `crypto::SHA256` (2, 32 bytes), `crypto::SHA384` (3, 48 bytes) or `crypto::SHA512` (4, 64 bytes). `crypto::aes_key(key const byte[:]) crypto::Aes` builds the key schedule for a key of 16, 24 or 32 bytes. `dcrypt::dc_aes_dec(a const crypto::Aes&, inp const byte[:], o byte[16]&)` decrypts the 16 bytes at the start of inp into o (one block, no chaining, no IV).

`OC_URI` is the text "http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/2006/keyEncryptor/password". The three Agile block keys are module `let` arrays of 8 bytes: `OC_BK_VIN` is 0xFE 0xA7 0xD2 0x76 0x3B 0x4B 0x9E 0x79, `OC_BK_VHASH` is 0xD7 0xAA 0x0F 0x6D 0x30 0x61 0x34 0x4E and `OC_BK_KEY` is 0x14 0x6E 0x0B 0xE7 0xAB 0xAC 0xD0 0xD6.

The Agile descriptor's values live in module variables that `oc_params` sets: `var oc_dh = 0` (the hash kind of the key data) and `var oc_dbits = 0` (its key size in bits), `var oc_dsalt byte[>..]` (its salt); `var oc_kh = 0` (the hash kind of the password key encryptor), `var oc_kbits = 0`, `var oc_spin_n = 0` (its spin count), `var oc_ksalt byte[>..]` (its salt), `var oc_vin byte[>..]` (the encrypted verifier hash input), `var oc_vhash byte[>..]` (the encrypted verifier hash value) and `var oc_kval byte[>..]` (the encrypted key value).

`oc_fit(d const byte[:], n int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends exactly n bytes: the first n bytes of d when d is longer, otherwise d followed by as many 0x36 bytes as needed.

`oc_pw16(pw const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends the UTF-8 text pw as UTF-16 little endian: each well-formed UTF-8 sequence of 1 to 4 bytes becomes its code point, a code point above 0xFFFF becomes a surrogate pair (high surrogate first, each unit written low byte first), and a byte that does not start a well-formed sequence (a stray continuation byte, an overlong or truncated sequence, a code point above 0x10FFFF or a surrogate) becomes U+FFFD and consumes only that one byte.

`oc_num(s const byte[:]) int` is the value of s read as plain decimal digits, or -1 when s is empty, longer than 9 bytes or holds anything but the digits 0 to 9.

`oc_hkind(name const byte[:]) int` is `crypto::SHA1` for the exact text "SHA1", `crypto::SHA256` for "SHA256", `crypto::SHA384` for "SHA384", `crypto::SHA512` for "SHA512" and 0 for anything else.

`oc_aeq(i int, name const byte[:], want const byte[:]) bool` is true when element i has an attribute named name (through `xml::xm_attr`) whose value equals want exactly.

`oc_an(i int, name const byte[:]) int` is `oc_num` of the value of attribute name of element i, or -1 when the attribute is missing.

`oc_ab(i int, name const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` clears out, then returns true when element i has the attribute name and its value is base64 that `base64::decode` accepts, leaving the decoded bytes in out; otherwise it returns false with out empty.

`oc_hk(i int) int` is `oc_hkind` of the hashAlgorithm attribute of element i (0 when it is missing or unknown).

`oc_cbc(a const crypto::Aes&, iv const byte[:], d const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` APPENDS to out the AES-CBC decryption of the whole 16-byte blocks of d (a trailing partial block is ignored), without removing any padding: each plaintext block is the `dcrypt::dc_aes_dec` result of the cipher block XORed with the previous cipher block, the first one with the first 16 bytes of iv.

`oc_ecb(a const crypto::Aes&, d const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` APPENDS to out the AES-ECB decryption of the whole 16-byte blocks of d (a trailing partial block is ignored), each block through `dcrypt::dc_aes_dec`.

`oc_spin(kind int, salt const byte[:], pw16 const byte[:], count int) byte[>..]` is the iterated password hash of [MS-OFFCRYPTO] with the hash kind: start with `crypto::digest` of the salt followed by pw16; then count times, for the iterator i running from 0 to count minus 1, replace the hash by `crypto::digest` of i as 4 little-endian bytes followed by the current hash; return the hash.

`oc_bkey(kind int, h const byte[:], blk const byte[:], n int, out byte[>..]&)` clears out and puts there the key for one purpose: the `crypto::digest` of h followed by the block key blk, made exactly n bytes with `oc_fit`.

`oc_size(pkg const byte[:]) int` is the plain size an encrypted package claims: the 8-byte little-endian number at the start of pkg (`cfb::cf_le`); it is -1 when pkg has fewer than 8 bytes, when that number is above 2147483647 or when it is more than the bytes after the first 8 (the stream was cut short).

`oc_cut(out byte[>..]&, sz int) bool` cuts out down to its first sz bytes and returns true; when out holds fewer than sz bytes (the decryption produced less than the package claims) it clears out and returns false.

`oc_params(info const byte[:]) bool` reads an Agile EncryptionInfo stream (the XML descriptor starts after its first 8 bytes) into the descriptor variables and returns true only when everything needed is present and supported; it returns false for anything else. Call `xml::xm_parse` on the bytes after the first 8 (nothing to parse when there are fewer than 9). Find the first keyData element with `xml::xm_find` over all events. Find the password key encryptor: the first keyEncryptor element whose uri attribute equals `OC_URI` (`oc_aeq`), looking through every keyEncryptor element in turn with `xml::xm_find`, and its encryptedKey child (`xml::xm_child`); either missing means false. For each of the two elements, keyData and encryptedKey, require: the cipherAlgorithm attribute equals "AES", the cipherChaining attribute equals "ChainingModeCBC", the blockSize attribute is 16 (`oc_an`), the keyBits attribute is 128, 192 or 256, the hash kind `oc_hk` is not 0, and the saltValue attribute decodes with `oc_ab` into at least 1 and at most 1024 bytes. Also require that the spinCount attribute of encryptedKey is from 0 to 10000000, and that encryptedVerifierHashInput, encryptedVerifierHashValue and encryptedKeyValue of encryptedKey each decode with `oc_ab` into a non-empty whole number of 16-byte blocks. Store: `oc_dh`, `oc_dbits` and `oc_dsalt` from keyData; `oc_kh`, `oc_kbits`, `oc_spin_n`, `oc_ksalt`, `oc_vin`, `oc_vhash` and `oc_kval` from encryptedKey.

`oc_ag_key(pw16 const byte[:], key byte[>..]&) int` checks a password against the Agile descriptor left by `oc_params` and finds the package key: it returns 1 with the key in `key` (cleared first), 0 when the password is wrong and -1 when the descriptor's data is too short to check. Let h be `oc_spin` with `oc_kh`, `oc_ksalt`, pw16 and `oc_spin_n`, iv be `oc_fit` of `oc_ksalt` to 16 bytes, and for each purpose let its key be `oc_bkey` with `oc_kh`, h, the block key and `oc_kbits` divided by 8 bytes, turned into a schedule with `crypto::aes_key`. Verifier input: `oc_cbc` of `oc_vin` with the key for `OC_BK_VIN` and iv, and keep its first (length of `oc_ksalt`) bytes, failing with -1 when it has fewer. Verifier hash: `oc_cbc` of `oc_vhash` with the key for `OC_BK_VHASH` and iv, and keep its first (digest size of `oc_kh`) bytes, failing with -1 when it has fewer. When `crypto::digest` of `oc_kh` over the verifier input is not equal to the verifier hash byte for byte, return 0. Otherwise `oc_cbc` of `oc_kval` with the key for `OC_BK_KEY` and iv, cut to its first `oc_dbits` divided by 8 bytes (-1 when it has fewer) is the key; return 1.

`oc_ag_data(key const byte[:], pkg const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) int` decrypts an Agile EncryptedPackage stream: it clears out and returns 1 with the plain package in out, or -1 (out empty) when `oc_size` of pkg is negative. The encrypted data is pkg after its first 8 bytes, split into segments of 4096 bytes (the last one may be shorter). Build the key schedule once with `crypto::aes_key`. For segment number s counting from 0, the iv is `oc_fit` to 16 bytes of `crypto::digest` with `oc_dh` of `oc_dsalt` followed by s as 4 little-endian bytes; append `oc_cbc` of the segment with that iv to out. At the end `oc_cut` out to the size `oc_size` gave; when that returns false the result is -1.

`oc_agile(info const byte[:], pkg const byte[:], pw16 const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) int` is the Agile path: it returns -1 when `oc_params` of info is false; otherwise `oc_ag_key` of pw16 into a local key, and when that is not 1 it returns that result (0 or -1); otherwise it returns `oc_ag_data` of the key and pkg into out.

`oc_std(info const byte[:], pkg const byte[:], pw16 const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) int` is the Standard path (the layout of [MS-OFFCRYPTO] 2.3.4.5): it returns 1 with the plain package in out, 0 for a wrong password, -1 for unsupported or damaged input, and clears out first. All numbers below are little-endian numbers from info through `cfb::cf_le`, every one of them must be at least 0 (an unreadable number means -1). The stream holds, after the 4 version bytes, the flags (4 bytes at offset 4) and the header size hs (4 bytes at offset 8); the encryption header starts at offset 12 and has its algorithm id at offset 20, hash id at offset 24 and key size in bits at offset 28; the verifier starts at v, which is 12 plus hs. Require in the flags both the CryptoAPI bit 0x04 and the AES bit 0x20 and not the external bit 0x10; hs from 32 to 65536; the algorithm id 0x660E with key size 128, or 0x660F with 192, or 0x6610 with 256; the hash id 0x8004 (SHA-1) or 0; the salt size at v equal to 16; the verifier hash size at v plus 36 equal to 20; and that v plus 72 is not past the end of info. The salt is the 16 bytes at v plus 4, the encrypted verifier the 16 bytes at v plus 20 and the encrypted verifier hash the 32 bytes at v plus 40. Key: let h be `crypto::digest` with `crypto::SHA1` of the salt followed by pw16; then for i from 0 to 49999 replace h by the SHA-1 of i as 4 little-endian bytes followed by h; then h is the SHA-1 of h followed by 4 zero bytes; build two 64-byte blocks, the first of 0x36 bytes and the second of 0x5C bytes, XOR the 20 bytes of h into the start of each, and take the SHA-1 of each block; the key is the first (key size divided by 8) bytes of the first digest followed by the second digest. With the schedule of that key (`crypto::aes_key`): the verifier is `oc_ecb` of the encrypted verifier, the verifier hash is the first 20 bytes of `oc_ecb` of the encrypted verifier hash, and when the SHA-1 of the verifier differs from the verifier hash return 0. Then `oc_size` of pkg must not be negative (otherwise -1); the plain package is `oc_ecb` of pkg after its first 8 bytes appended to out, then `oc_cut` of out to that size (-1 when that returns false); return 1.

`oc_is_encrypted(d const byte[:]) bool` is true when d is a compound file (`cfb::cf_open` gives a handle) that has both a stream "EncryptionInfo" and a stream "EncryptedPackage" (`cfb::cf_find` finds each at the root and `cfb::cf_is_stream` is true for it); it closes the handle before returning.

`oc_decrypt(d const byte[:], pw const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) int` decrypts the compound file d with the UTF-8 password pw. It clears out first and returns 1 with the decrypted package (a ZIP) in out, 0 for a wrong password (out stays empty), and -1 for anything unsupported, damaged or not an encrypted Office file (out stays empty); it never fails on garbage. Open d with `cfb::cf_open` (-1 when that fails); read the streams "EncryptionInfo" and "EncryptedPackage" into two local lists with `cfb::cf_get` (-1 when either fails), and close the handle right after reading. The version is the 2-byte numbers at offsets 0 (major) and 2 (minor) of the info (`cfb::cf_le`; -1 when the info has fewer than 8 bytes): 4 and 4 means Agile, so return `oc_agile` of the info, the package, `oc_pw16` of pw and out; a major version of 2, 3 or 4 with minor 2 means Standard, so return `oc_std` the same way; any other version gives -1. When the result is not 1, make sure out is empty before returning.
